---
/**
 * Random Kit tool page.
 */
import ToolLayout from '../../layouts/ToolLayout.astro';
import RandomKit from '../../components/tools/RandomKit';
---

<ToolLayout slug="random-kit">
  <RandomKit client:load />

  <Fragment slot="about">
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p>
      Every random generator on the web hides its source, which leaves the only two questions anyone
      has unanswered. Can I get these numbers back tomorrow? And is this safe to use as a password?
    </p>
    <p>
      Those questions have opposite answers, and no single source satisfies both.
      <code>Math.random()</code>, which is what nearly all of these tools use, satisfies neither: it
      cannot be seeded, so nothing is reproducible, and it is not cryptographically secure, so
      nothing is safe. It is the worst of both and it is the default.
    </p>

    <h2>The approach</h2>
    <p>
      The source is the first control on the page. Seeded mode is xoshiro128**, a small well-studied
      generator driven by a hash of your seed: same seed, same settings, same output on any machine,
      which is what makes generated data usable as a test fixture. System mode is
      <code>crypto.getRandomValues</code>, seeded by the operating system, suitable for a token and
      impossible to reproduce. Both are labelled, and the UI says so again next to generated strings
      if the seeded source is the one producing them.
    </p>
    <p>
      A few implementation choices are the difference between correct output and plausible output.
      Unique integer draws switch strategy on density: rejection sampling while the range is much
      wider than the count, and a partial Fisher-Yates shuffle once the request wants more than a
      third of the available integers, because rejection sampling on a nearly-full range spends
      almost all its time rejecting. Shuffling is Fisher-Yates rather than
      <code>sort(() =&gt; Math.random() - 0.5)</code>, which is measurably biased and extremely
      common. UUIDs set the version and variant bits, so the result is a well-formed v4 rather than
      sixteen random bytes with hyphens in it.
    </p>
    <p>
      The histogram is there because a list of numbers all looks equally random. The shape is where
      a misread option or a bad generator shows itself, and it is the only way to confirm the
      distribution setting did what it said.
    </p>

    <h2>Where it stops being trustworthy</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Seeded output is not secret.</strong> Anyone with the seed can reproduce every value.
        That is the feature. It also means the seeded mode must never generate a password, and the page
        says so where it could matter.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The non-uniform distributions are clamped to the range.</strong> Normal and exponential
        are unbounded, so values past either end are pinned rather than redrawn. That piles a little probability
        onto the endpoints instead of distorting the interior, and each distribution&rsquo;s note says
        so. The triangular shape needs no clamping and has none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Unique floats are close to meaningless.</strong> Rounded to a few decimal places, collisions
        are common, and the tool reports how many it dropped rather than quietly returning a shorter list.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>The entropy figure assumes a uniform source.</strong> True in system mode. In seeded mode
        the number describes the alphabet, not the unpredictability, because there is none.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One draw is capped at 100,000 values.</strong> Past that a browser tab is the wrong tool
        and the shell is the right one.
      </li>
    </ul>
  </Fragment>
</ToolLayout>
